Source package: xdg-desktop-portal

inhibit-export-request-after-backend.patch
    Keeps an application which withdraws an inhibition right after asking for it from leaving the inhibition behind.
    Applications inhibit a logout, a suspend or idling through the Inhibit portal, and withdraw the inhibition by
    closing the request the portal returned, as GTK does in gtk_application_uninhibit(). xdg-desktop-portal answered
    Inhibit at once, and called the backend, xdg-desktop-portal-gtk on GNOME, only afterwards, from a thread, once it
    had looked up the application's permissions. A Close which arrived in between was forwarded to the backend before
    the backend had created its request, and failed with "Object does not exist at path". The backend then still asked
    gnome-session for the inhibition, which stayed until the application disconnected from the bus. Text Editor, when it
    does not restore its session, inhibits for each window with unsaved documents, and loading a file marks the document
    modified and unmodified again, so opening a file inhibited and withdrew right away, and GNOME Shell's logout dialog
    listed Text Editor once more as having unsaved work.

    The handler of Inhibit runs in a thread of its own, and now calls the backend synchronously, and exports the request
    and answers only once the backend has returned, which it does after it has exported its own request. A Close then
    always finds the backend's request. The handler holds the request's lock meanwhile, so an application which
    disconnects in between has its request closed in the backend once the handler is done. The application also receives
    the reply to Inhibit only once the backend has the inhibition, so an application which inhibits when the session is
    about to end, and calls QueryEndResponse once Inhibit returned, has the backend receive the two calls in that order,
    and the session cannot end without the inhibition.

    Reported upstream as https://github.com/flatpak/xdg-desktop-portal/issues/854, where a maintainer suggested this
    fix, and the order of Inhibit and QueryEndResponse as https://github.com/flatpak/xdg-desktop-portal/issues/1881.
    https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gnome-session/-/work_items/174 reports the same symptom with Nautilus. The patch also
    changes the test suite, which the build runs: the mock backend now returns from Inhibit when it expects a Close, as
    xdg-desktop-portal-gtk does, instead of returning only on Close. A new test closes a request right after Inhibit
    returned, with the mock backend's delay set to 200 ms, so that it handles the call slowly, and another one, in 5
    rounds, calls QueryEndResponse right after Inhibit returned and checks that the backend received Inhibit first. Both
    fail without the fix. Proposed upstream as https://github.com/flatpak/xdg-desktop-portal/pull/2199. Drop the patch
    once Ubuntu's xdg-desktop-portal contains the fix.

    The fix is in handle_inhibit in src/inhibit.c. The session test portal-inhibit in tests/session/ checks it in a
    GNOME session: 200 requests closed right after Inhibit have to leave no inhibitor behind. Without the patch, 116 of
    200 were left behind.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login, after opening a few files with gnome-text-editor --standalone, which
    does not restore its session, without changing them:

      gnome-session-inhibit --list    should list Text Editor only once for each window with unsaved documents
